th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/one-sided-limit.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/one-sided-limit.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the notation $\\lim_{x\\to x_0^+} f(x) = L$ mean?","options":["$f(x)$ approaches $L$ as $x$ approaches $x_0$ from the left.","$f(x)$ approaches $L$ as $x$ approaches $x_0$ from the right.","$f(x)$ approaches $L$ as $x$ approaches $x_0$ from either side.","$f(x)$ approaches $L$ as $x$ increases without bound."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b65d01305d9dd37f41768163","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a right-hand limit at $x_0$, which points are used in the epsilon-delta condition?","options":["Points satisfying $x_0-\\delta \\lt x \\lt x_0$.","Only the point $x = x_0$.","Only points outside $(x_0-r,x_0+r)$.","Points satisfying $x_0 \\lt x \\lt x_0+\\delta$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f6c053b0e372b11db26e4d9","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For the same function with $f(0) = 99$, $f(x) = 1$ for $x \\gt 0$, and $f(x) = -1$ for $x \\lt 0$, which statement is true about the two-sided limit at $0$?","options":["It equals $0$, the average of the one-sided limits.","It equals $1$, because limits are taken from the right.","It does not exist, because the one-sided limits differ.","It equals $99$, because a limit is the function value $f(0)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91f2b4d13b362f506920ffa6","time_limit":40},{"id":"one_sided_limit_new_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which notation also represents a right-hand limit at $x_0$?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\uparrow x_0}f(x)$","$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}f(x)$","$\\lim_{x\\downarrow x_0}f(x)$","$f'(x_0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a89e5f7b4e798bfea9788215","time_limit":20},{"id":"one_sided_limit_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f(x)=3$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f(x)=3$. What follows?","options":["$f(a)=3$ necessarily","The two-sided limit does not exist","$\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=3$","$f$ is differentiable at $a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c062115f02a921a9bfbccc74","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=1$ for $x>0$ and $f(x)=\\sin(1/x)$ for $x<0$. Which statement about the one-sided limits at $0$ is true?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)=1$, and $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)=1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)=0$.","Both one-sided limits exist, because $f$ is bounded near $0$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist, because $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ad0764a0574f61738668c7d","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_007_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(0)=99$, $f(x)=1$ for $x>0$, and $f(x)=-1$ for $x<0$. What is $\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)$?","options":["$99$","$-1$","$1$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8bc5cafb435dad815f1dcb0c","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_012_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x+2$ for $x>1$ and $f(x)=7$ for $x\\le1$. Which statement is true at $1$?","options":["The left-hand limit is $3$ and the right-hand limit is $7$.","Both one-sided limits are $7$.","The left-hand limit is $7$ and the right-hand limit is $3$.","Both one-sided limits are $3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"abc955a3e92d34e2aaf6ab90","time_limit":35},{"id":"one_sided_limit_new_016_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=1/x$ for $x\\ne0$. Which statement about the one-sided limits at $0$ is true?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty$.","No one-sided limit can be stated, because $f(0)$ is undefined."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"86eb0321c1a663a8b00832c9","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ with domain $[0,\\infty)$. Which statement about limits of $f$ at $x_0=0$ is correct?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)=0$ as well, because $f(0)=0$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0}f(x)$ does not exist, because the left-hand limit does not exist.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=0$, while a left-hand limit at $0$ is not defined.","No limit at $0$ can be formed, because $0$ is an endpoint of the domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"692861b44ebbb83d8489adb8","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_one_sided_limit_4cb563e4_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{1/x}$ for $x\\ne0$. Which statement about the one-sided limits of $f$ at $0$ is correct?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=+\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)=+\\infty$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=+\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)=-\\infty$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)=+\\infty$.","$\\lim_{x\\to0^+}f(x)=+\\infty$ and $\\lim_{x\\to0^-}f(x)=0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a7f224645494c61df6ac5d3","time_limit":30}]}}